['Here is a basic C implementation of a semaphore management library supporting `acquire`, `release` and `destroy` operations. This does not take into account all of your requirements but it should be a good starting point for you. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct Sem {
    sem_t mutex;
    int value;
} Sem;

void init(Sem* sem, int val) {
    if (sem_init(&sem->mutex, 0, val) == -1) {
        perror("Failed to initialize the semaphore");
        return;
    }
    sem->value = val;
}

void acquire(Sem* sem) {
    if (sem_wait(&sem->mutex) < 0) {
        perror("Failed to wait on a semaphore");
        return;
    }
    sem->value--;
}

void release(Sem* sem) {
    if (sem_post(&sem->mutex) < 0) {
        perror("Failed to signal a semaphore");
        return;
    }
    sem->value++;
}

void destroy(Sem* sem) {
    if (sem_destroy(&sem->mutex) == -1) {
        perror("Failed to destroy the semaphore");
        return;
    }
}
```

This is a basic implementation of a semaphore in C. It uses `pthread` and `semaphore.h` libraries for thread synchronization and error handling. The `init()` function initializes a semaphore with a given value, the `acquire()` function waits on a semaphore if it is unavailable, the `release()` function signals a semaphore to increment its value allowing other threads waiting on it to continue execution and finally, the `destroy()` function deallocates any resources used by the semaphore.

This code does not take into account advanced error handling or performance optimizations which you require for a robust solution. For these features, I would recommend studying operating system concepts like mutexes, condition variables, deadlock prevention, and other concurrent programming topics as well as C/C++ multithreading libraries. It is also advisable to use automated testing tools like Google Test or Boost Test to ensure your code works as expected under different conditions.
