['The task described is complex and might involve multiple sub-tasks like handling environment variables, directory creation/management, xml parsing with XPath, error handling etc. Here's a simplified version of the code to handle the XML file parsing using XPath. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Xml.XPath;
using System.Threading;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;

public class Program
{
    private static ConcurrentDictionary<string, string> _parsedFiles = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, string>();

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Enter an element name to search in XML files:");
        var elementName = Console.ReadLine();

        // Check if environment variable is set
        var xmlDirectoryPath = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("XML_DIR");
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(xmlDirectoryPath))
        {
            xmlDirectoryPath = $"{AppDomain.CurrentDomain.BaseDirectory}/XmlFiles";

            // Create directory if not exists
            Directory.CreateDirectory(xmlDirectoryPath);

            // Setting the environment variable
            Environment.SetEnvironmentVariable("XML_DIR", xmlDirectoryPath, EnvironmentVariableTarget.User);
        }

        var files = Directory.GetFiles(xmlDirectoryPath, "*.xml");

        foreach (var file in files)
        {
            ThreadPool.QueueUserWorkItem(_ => ParseXmlFile(file, elementName));
        }
    }

    private static void ParseXmlFile(object fileObj, object elementNameObj)
    {
        var file = (string)fileObj;
        var elementName = (string)elementNameObj;

        // Check if file is already parsed
        if (!_parsedFiles.ContainsKey(file))
        {
            try
            {
                // Load the XML document
                XPathDocument doc = new XPathDocument(file);

                // Create an XPathNavigator object from the document
                XPathNavigator navigator = doc.CreateNavigator();

                // Select nodes using XPath
                XPathNodeIterator iterator = navigator.SelectDescendants(elementName, "", false);

                // Iterate through results
                while (iterator.MoveNext())
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"{file}: Found element '{elementName}'");
                    break;
                }

                _parsedFiles[file] = file;
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Error in parsing the XML file: {ex}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```
The code above will parse all the XML files in a given directory and find if an element exists or not. If environment variable `XML_DIR` is set, it uses that path otherwise, it creates a new folder called `XmlFiles` under current directory and sets the environment variable to that path. It also checks if file has already been parsed before, so as to avoid parsing multiple times for same files in different threads.

Please note that handling large XML files and managing permissions are beyond this simplified snippet. You would need additional code to manage these features. Handling namespaces, varying schemas and conflicts is also a complex task that needs careful implementation. Optimizing the function for frequently accessed files and handling high concurrency loads requires proper consideration of thread synchronization strategies. Error handling is rudimentary here; in production scenarios, comprehensive error handling would be required. 

This example does not use Xml Namespaces or attributes to resolve conflicts as it's not clear how one might do so without knowing the structure and contents of your XML files. You can apply this based on your requirements and logic. Also, note that `XPathNodeIterator` will return only first match if multiple matches are found.
']

['Here's an example that shows how to write such a method in Java using Apache Commons Lang and Google's Guava library:

```java
import com.google.common.hash.Hashing;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.builder.ToStringBuilder;

import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class JsonObjectHasher {
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> jsonCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static String hashJson(String json) throws IllegalArgumentException{
        if (json == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("JSON string cannot be null");

        return jsonCache.computeIfAbsent(json, j -> Hashing.sha256().hashString(j, StandardCharsets.UTF_8).toString());
    }

    public static String hashObject(Object obj) {
        if (obj == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("JSON object cannot be null");

        return hashJson(ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(obj));
    }
}
```
This code provides a `hashJson` and `hashObject` method that computes the SHA-256 hash of a JSON string using Guava's `Hashing.sha256()` function, which is then converted to a String for storage in the cache. The `ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(obj)` call handles null values and circular references by converting the object to its canonical form in Java, automatically avoiding infinite recursion and correctly handling arrays and collections.

To handle concurrency control, we use the thread-safe `ConcurrentHashMap` class from Java's standard library which allows multiple threads to safely read data without synchronization overhead. The method `computeIfAbsent()` ensures that if a JSON string has been previously hashed before, it will not be rehashed again thus saving memory and computation resources.

However, please note that this solution doesn't handle the edge case of very large objects. This could be mitigated by applying some compression technique (like GZIP) on the input JSON string before hashing or simply rejecting such inputs. 

For a realistic stress test scenario, one should use testing frameworks like JMeter for load testing and Apache Bench for quick, simple tests. It would be best to create a suite of tests that simulate real-world usage scenarios as accurately as possible. This includes testing under concurrent load to determine the system's capacity and its behavior when there is sudden spike in requests.
']

