["Here's a basic example of how you might implement such a function:

```javascript
function traverseDOM(selector, attributes) {
  const stack = [document.body];
  const elements = [];

  while (stack.length > 0) {
    let node = stack.pop();

    if (!node || !node.style || getComputedStyle(node).display === 'none') {
      continue;
    }

    if (typeof selector === 'string' && 
        node.matches && 
        node.matches(selector)) {

      let shouldPush = true;

      for (let attr in attributes) {
        if (!node.hasAttribute(attr) || 
            node.getAttribute(attr) !== attributes[attr]) {

          shouldPush = false;
          break;
        }
      }

      if (shouldPush) {
        elements.push(node);
      }
    }

    for (let i = node.children.length - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
      stack.push(node.children[i]);
    }
  }

  return elements;
}
```
This function uses a depth-first search approach to traverse the DOM tree, which is suitable for this use case as it ensures efficient handling of deeply nested elements. It also avoids processing hidden elements by checking their computed style display property. The `matches` and `getAttribute` methods are used instead of direct equality checks or element properties to handle potential conflicts with existing scripts.

Error handling is minimal in this example, but could be expanded upon based on specific requirements or constraints. For instance, you might want to throw exceptions for missing selectors or malformed attributes to make debugging easier.

Note: This function could lead to performance issues and stack overflow errors when dealing with very large DOM trees or deeply nested elements due to the use of recursion. A more efficient solution would be a breadth-first search using an iterative approach, batch processing updates, or deferring operations until a less critical time as mentioned in your problem statement.
"]

