["Here's a rough outline of how you might approach this problem using Java NIO channels for managing connections. However, writing an entire system in one block would be impractical due to its complexity and size. This code just provides a skeleton structure on how you could start implementing such a service.

Please note that actual implementation will require detailed design, error handling, testing, and more. Also, this is a simplified solution for teaching purposes only:

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.channels.SelectionKey;
import java.nio.channels.Selector;
import java.nio.channels.ServerSocketChannel;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Set;

public class ConnectionPoolService {
    private Selector selector;
    private ServerSocketChannel serverSocketChannel;

    public ConnectionPoolService(int port) throws IOException {
        this.selector = Selector.open();
        this.serverSocketChannel = ServerSocketChannel.open();

        serverSocketChannel.socket().bind(new InetSocketAddress(port));
        serverSocketChannel.configureBlocking(false);

        SelectionKey selectionKey = serverSocketChannel.register(selector, SelectionKey.OP_ACCEPT);
        selectionKey.attach(new Acceptor());
    }

    public void run() {
        try {
            while (!Thread.interrupted()) {
                selector.select();

                Set<SelectionKey> selectedKeys = selector.selectedKeys();
                Iterator<SelectionKey> iterator = selectedKeys.iterator();

                while (iterator.hasNext()) {
                    SelectionKey key = iterator.next();
                    dispatch(key);

                    iterator.remove();
                }
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // handle exception
        }
    }

    private void dispatch(SelectionKey key) {
        Runnable attachment = (Runnable) key.attachment();
        attachment.run();
    }

    private class Acceptor implements Runnable {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            // handle new connection
        }
    }
}
```
In this code:
- We open a ServerSocketChannel and attach an Acceptor to it. The SelectionKey is registered for OP_ACCEPT events which are triggered when there's a new incoming connection. 
- The run method handles incoming events from the selector, dispatching them to their respective handlers (which are attached to the keys). 
- The `Acceptor` just represents a basic handler of new connections and is implemented as an inner class for simplicity. In a real application, this would be more complex: it would handle reading/writing data from/to the connection, adjusting the pool size based on current metrics etc.

This is a very simplified example which lacks many features you've requested (like dynamic adjustment of the pool size, handling transient failures, optimizing resource usage etc.). But it gives an idea about how to start with this kind of system. You would need to expand it by adding more handlers for different types of events, managing connection pools, implementing metrics monitoring and adjusting them dynamically etc.
"]

