```javascript
function traverseAndSelectDOMElements(startElem, callback) {
    let matchingElements = [];

    function traverseDOMTree(elem) {
        if (callback(elem)) {
            matchingElements.push(elem);
        }

        elem.childNodes.forEach(child => {
            if (child.nodeType === 1 && child !== startElem) { // only elements, not the starting element
                traverseDOMTree(child);
            }
        });
    }

    try {
        if (!document.body.contains(startElem)) {
            throw new Error('Starting element is not found in the DOM.');
        } else {
            traverseDOMTree(startElem);
        }
    } catch (error) {
        console.log(`Error: ${error}`);
    }

    return matchingElements;
}
```
In this JavaScript function, `callback` is a function that takes an element as argument and returns true if it should be included in the result array. It can be used to filter elements based on their class or ID by passing a function that checks these attributes. The error handling considers situations where the starting element isn't found in the DOM, indicating that the document might not be fully loaded yet.
