['The task you're asking is quite complex, which includes multiple aspects such as concurrent execution of background tasks, prioritized execution, handling failed processes, adaptive scaling, resource cleanup, integrating external monitoring tools etc. Here I provide a simplified example using POSIX threads and the Linux `nice` command to execute commands in background with priority. This won't solve all your problems but it gives you an idea on how to use these concepts:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <signal.h>

struct command {
    char *command;
    int priority;
};

void *execute_command(void *arg) {
    struct command *cmd = (struct command *) arg;
    nice(-cmd->priority);
    system(cmd->command);
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t threads[2];
    int i, rc;
    struct command cmds[2] = {{"ls", 10}, {"sleep 5", -20}}; // replace with your commands and priorities

    for (i=0; i < 2; i++) { // change to handle an arbitrary number of commands
        printf("Creating thread %d\n", i);
        rc = pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, execute_command, &cmds[i]);

        if (rc){
            fprintf(stderr, "ERROR; return code from pthread_create() is %d\n", rc);
            exit(-1);
        }
    }

    for (i=0; i < 2; i++) { // replace with handling an arbitrary number of commands
        if(pthread_join(threads[i], NULL)) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Error joining thread\n");
            return 1;
        }
    }

    printf("All threads joined successfully.\n");
    return 0;
}
```
This program creates a main function that spawns two child threads to execute the commands. The `execute_command` function takes care of the priority management by invoking `nice()`. 

