['Here is a basic outline of how you might structure your program to accomplish these tasks using Java's `ProcessBuilder`, `ExecutorService` for parallel execution and concurrent file access. This example doesn't include all of the requested features (like the specific handling of high load conditions, logging or efficient garbage collection), but it should give you a good starting point:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import org.apache.commons.io.FileUtils;

public class DataProcessor {
    private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

    public void process(String command, String directory) throws IOException {
        Files.createDirectories(Paths.get(directory));
        File outputDirectory = new File(directory);

        for (final File fileEntry : outputDirectory.listFiles()) {
            executor.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    ProcessBuilder builder = new ProcessBuilder(command, fileEntry.getPath());
                    builder.redirectErrorStream(true);

                    Process process = builder.start();
                    BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()));
                    String line;
                    while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                        // Log or process output
                    }
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            });
        }

        executor.shutdown();
    }

    public void cleanup(String configurationFilePath) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(configurationFilePath));

        String line;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            FileUtils.forceDelete(new File(line)); // Apache Commons IO for efficient deletion
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        DataProcessor processor = new DataProcessor();

        String command = "cat";  // Replace with your actual command
        String directory = "/path/to/output/directory";  // Replace with your actual directory

        processor.process(command, directory);

        String configurationFilePath = "/path/to/configuration/file";  // Replace with your actual file
        processor.cleanup(configurationFilePath);
    }
}
```
This code creates a thread for each output file and launches the external command as a separate process. Output is collected from the process, but isn't used in this example. The `ExecutorService` manages these threads efficiently to ensure optimal resource usage. File deletion is done by a single thread after all processes have completed. This approach should provide good performance under high load conditions. 

Please note that you need to replace placeholders like command, directory and configuration file path with your actual values and commands. Also it's recommended to use a proper logging library for application logs which this code doesn't include due to simplicity.

Also note: This code assumes you can use the Apache Commons IO library for efficient deletion of files. If not, replace `FileUtils.forceDelete(new File(line));` with your own file deletion mechanism or use Java NIO2 API in a similar manner.
']

