['The following C# code is a basic solution for your requirements using `System.IO` and `System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary` namespaces for binary serialization and `Parallel.ForEach` for parallel processing. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.IO.Compression;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class DataStructure
{
    // Fill this with your own custom data structure fields and methods
}

public static class LargeDataLoader
{
    private static readonly BinaryFormatter _formatter = new BinaryFormatter();

    public static List<DataStructure> LoadFromFile(string filePath, int bufferSize = 4096)
    {
        var resultList = new List<DataStructure>();

        using (var sourceStream = new FileStream(filePath, FileMode.Open))
        using (var zipStream = new GZipStream(sourceStream, CompressionMode.Decompress))
        {
            while (zipStream.Position < zipStream.Length)
            {
                byte[] buffer;

                // Handle memory usage to avoid out-of-memory exceptions
                if ((zipStream.Length - zipStream.Position) < bufferSize)
                {
                    buffer = new byte[zipStream.Length - zipStream.Position];
                }
                else
                {
                    buffer = new byte[bufferSize];
                }

                try
                {
                    // Reading a chunk of data from the compressed file
                    var bytesRead = zipStream.Read(buffer, 0, buffer.Length);

                    if (bytesRead == 0)
                    {
                        break;
                    }

                    using (var ms = new MemoryStream(buffer))
                    {
                        // Deserializing the data into our custom structure
                        var deserializedData = (DataStructure)_formatter.Deserialize(ms);

                        lock (resultList)
                        {
                            resultList.Add(deserializedData);
                        }
                    }
                }
                catch (SerializationException ex)
                {
                    // Handle deserialization errors gracefully
                    Console.WriteLine("Failed to deserialize data: " + ex.Message);
                }
            }
        }

        return resultList;
    }

    public static void LoadParallel(string[] filePaths)
    {
        // Using parallel processing for efficient large datasets
        Parallel.ForEach(filePaths, (currentFile) =>
        {
            try
            {
                var data = LoadFromFile(currentFile);
                Console.WriteLine("Successfully loaded " + currentFile);

                // Process the deserialized data as per your requirement
                // For instance: Save it to a database, perform some calculations...
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Failed to load file " + currentFile + ". Error details: " + ex.Message);
            }
        });
    }
}
``` 

This code can be extended for different compression formats by using other compression libraries such as `System.IO.Compression` or third-party ones like SharpZipLib. Regarding parallel processing strategies, you could use `Parallel.ForEach` to distribute the workload across multiple cores. But please consider that it might not always be faster than sequential operations due to synchronization overhead and the specifics of your data processing task.

The trade-offs in terms of performance, usability, and maintainability are very dependant on the specific use case, but for most common scenarios, this solution should work fine. Keep in mind that you might need to adjust the code to better suit your needs. For example, using a database or memory-mapped files could be more efficient than loading everything into memory at once, especially if the data doesn't fit into RAM.
