['Below is the Java code that implements these requirements:

```Java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class StateLoader {
    private static final String FILE_PATH = "path/to/file";
    private static final int MAX_CACHE_SIZE = 10;
    private static final long CACHE_EXPIRATION_TIME_MILLIS = 60 * 60 * 1000; // 1 hour

    private Map<String, StateCacheEntry> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private ScheduledExecutorService executorService = Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor();

    public StateLoader() {
        executorService.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> 
            cache.entrySet().removeIf(entry -> System.currentTimeMillis() > entry.getValue().getTimestamp()), 1, 1, TimeUnit.MINUTES);
    }

    public MoSTState loadStateFromFile(String filePath) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        if (cache.containsKey(filePath)) {
            StateCacheEntry entry = cache.get(filePath);

            if (System.currentTimeMillis() - entry.getTimestamp() > CACHE_EXPIRATION_TIME_MILLIS) {
                refreshFromFile(entry, filePath);
            }

            return entry.getState();
        } else {
            synchronized (cache) {
                if (!cache.containsKey(filePath)) {
                    StateCacheEntry entry = new StateCacheEntry();

                    refreshFromFile(entry, filePath);

                    cache.put(filePath, entry);

                    // Check the max cache size and remove old entries if needed
                    if (cache.size() > MAX_CACHE_SIZE) {
                        List<Map.Entry<String, StateCacheEntry>> entries = new ArrayList<>(cache.entrySet());

                        entries.sort((e1, e2) -> Long.compare(e1.getValue().getTimestamp(), e2.getValue().getTimestamp()));

                        cache.keySet().removeAll(entries.subList(0, entries.size() - MAX_CACHE_SIZE).stream().map(Map.Entry::getKey).collect(Collectors.toList()));
                    }
                }
            }

            return cache.get(filePath).getState();
        }
    }

    private void refreshFromFile(StateCacheEntry entry, String filePath) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            MoSTState state = (MoSTState) in.readObject();

            if (!state instanceof MoSTState) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid object type");
            }

            entry.setState(state);
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            // Handle exceptions
            System.err.println("Failed to load state from file: " + e.getMessage());
            throw e;
        } finally {
            cache.remove(filePath);
        }
    }

    private static class StateCacheEntry {
        private MoSTState state;
        private long timestamp = System.currentTimeMillis();

        public void setState(MoSTState state) {
            this.state = state;
        }

        public MoSTState getState() {
            return state;
        }

        public long getTimestamp() {
            return timestamp;
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses a combination of ConcurrentHashMap and Synchronized block for thread safety. The cache is checked before loading from file, if not found then only synchronization is used to make sure multiple threads are not reading or writing at the same time. For handling caching, it includes a scheduled job that removes old entries after they expire.
']

["Here is a sample code in Node.js that meets most of your requirements:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const crypto = require('crypto');

async function processFiles(directoryPath, limit) {
  const files = await getFilesRecursively(directoryPath);
  const queue = [];
  let inProgress = 0;

  for (let file of files) {
    while (inProgress >= limit) {
      // Wait until a slot frees up.
      await Promise.race(queue);
    }

    queue.push(processFile(file).catch((err) => console.error(`Failed to process ${file}: ${err}`)));
    if (queue.length > limit) {
      // If the queue is now over the limit, wait until a slot frees up.
      await Promise.race(queue);
    }
  }

  await Promise.all(queue);
  console.log('All files processed successfully');
}

function getFilesRecursively(directoryPath) {
  const dirents = fs.readdirSync(directoryPath, { withFileTypes: true });
  const files = dirents.map((dirent) => {
    const filePath = path.resolve(directoryPath, dirent.name);
    return dirent.isDirectory() ? getFilesRecursively(filePath) : filePath;
  });

  return Array.prototype.concat(...files);
}

async function processFile(filePath) {
  const data = await fs.promises.readFile(filePath, 'utf8');
  const uppercaseContent = data.toUpperCase();

  // Create a unique hash based on the original filename and current timestamp.
  const hash = crypto.createHash('md5').update(`${filePath}${Date.now()}`).digest('hex');
  const newFilePath = `${path.dirname(filePath)}/processed-${hash}-${path.basename(filePath)}`;

  await fs.promises.writeFile(newFilePath, uppercaseContent);
}
```

In this script:
1. `getFilesRecursively()` traverses the directory structure recursively to get all files.
2. For each file found, a promise is pushed into the queue and immediately awaited if there are too many tasks in progress (i.e., more than limit). This ensures we don't exceed our concurrency limit. 
3. Each task pushes its own promise onto `queue` and will run as soon as it can, so this part optimizes for disk I/O operations by processing files while waiting for others to complete.
4. Once all promises in the queue are done (i.e., all tasks have completed or failed), we print a success message.
5. `processFile()` reads each file, converts its content to uppercase, and writes it back with a new name. The new name is a hash of the original filename and current timestamp to avoid conflicts. 
6. This design allows for scalability as it processes files one at a time in batches, preventing excessive memory usage. Each task is independent from others, hence they can run concurrently without interference.
7. If any file processing fails, we log an error message but continue with the remaining tasks.
8. It uses `fs/promises` for efficient file operations and leverages Promises to handle asynchronous operations and control their execution order efficiently. 
9. However, this script does not provide detailed logging, and doesn't implement a system for handling name conflicts when files have the same modified filename. Further improvements can be made based on your specific requirements and constraints.
"]

