['Here's a simplified version of how you might implement this in PHP with the help of PDO for database handling. This is quite complex topic and requires a lot more details such as database connection configuration, clinic details API etc. The following code only gives an idea on how to approach this task. 

```php
<?php

function updateClinicStatus(array $clinicIds) {
    // Assuming you have established your PDO connection and stored it in $db
    $db = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=test', $user, $pass);

    $logFile = 'error.log';
    $status = 1;  // or whatever status you want to set
    $lastModifiedIP = $_SERVER['REMOTE_ADDR'];
    $lastModifiedDate = date('Y-m-d H:i:s');

    try {
        // Start transaction
        $db->beginTransaction();

        foreach($clinicIds as $id) {
            $stmt = $db->prepare("UPDATE clinics 
                                  SET status = :status, 
                                      last_modified_ip = :lastModifiedIP,
                                      last_modified_date = :lastModifiedDate 
                                WHERE id = :id");

            $stmt->bindParam(':status', $status);
            $stmt->bindParam(':lastModifiedIP', $lastModifiedIP);
            $stmt->bindParam(':lastModifiedDate', $lastModifiedDate);
            $stmt->bindParam(':id', $id);

            // Execute the statement
            $result = $stmt->execute();

            if ($result === false) {
                $errorInfo = implode(" ", $stmt->errorInfo());
                error_log($errorInfo, 3, $logFile);
                throw new Exception('Query execution failed: '.$errorInfo);
            }
        }

        $db->commit(); // commit the transaction
    } catch (Exception $e) {
        $db->rollBack(); // Rollback the transaction on error
        error_log($e->getMessage(), 3, $logFile);
        throw new Exception('Transaction failed: '.$e->getMessage());
    }
}
?>
```
This function updates a status, last modified IP address and date for each clinic ID in the array. It rolls back the transaction if any error occurs during query execution or transaction commit. Error logging is done to a file named 'error.log'. 

