['Creating this function requires knowledge of PHP, specifically with its database operations using PDO or MySQLi interfaces and AWS S3 SDK, as well as understanding of how image resizing works. 

```php
<?php
require 'vendor/autoload.php'; // include the AWS SDK
use Aws\S3\S3Client;

// define the dimensions constraints, file size limit and type constraints
const MAX_WIDTH = 800;
const MAX_HEIGHT = 600;
const MAX_SIZE = 2097152; // 2 MB
const ALLOWED_TYPES = ['image/jpeg', 'image/png'];

// define the S3 bucket name and region
$bucketName = "your-s3-bucket-name";
$region = "us-west-1";

function updateUserProfilePicture($userId, $imageFile) {
    // Check file size limit
    if ($imageFile['size'] > MAX_SIZE) {
        throw new Exception('Image is too large');
    }

    // Validate image type
    $finfo = finfo_open(FILEINFO_MIME_TYPE);
    $mimeType = finfo_file($finfo, $imageFile['tmp_name']);
    if (!in_array($mimeType, ALLOWED_TYPES)) {
        throw new Exception('Image type not allowed');
    }

    // Resize the image to match constraints
    $img = imagecreatefromstring(file_get_contents($imageFile['tmp_name']));
    $width = imagesx($img);
    $height = imagesy($img);
    if ($width > MAX_WIDTH || $height > MAX_HEIGHT) {
        // calculate the aspect ratio
        $aspectRatio = $width / $height;

        // resize to fit within constraints
        if ($width / $height > $maxSize['x'] / $maxSize['y']) {
            $newWidth = round($maxSize['x']);
            $newHeight = round($newWidth / $aspectRatio);
        } else {
            $newHeight = round($maxSize['y']);
            $newWidth = round($newHeight * $aspectRatio);
        }

        $resizedImg = imagecreatetruecolor($newWidth, $newHeight);
        imagecopyresampled($resizedImg, $img, 0, 0, 0, 0, $newWidth, $newHeight, $width, $height);

        $imageFile['tmp_name'] = $this->saveImageToTemp($resizedImg, $mimeType); // save the resized image to a temporary file
    }

    try {
        // initialize S3 client with your credentials and region
        $s3 = new Aws\S3\S3Client([
            'version' => 'latest',
            'region'  => $region,
            'credentials' => [
                'key'    => "your-aws-access-key",
                'secret' => "your-aws-secret-key"
            ]
        ]);

        $result = $s3->putObject([
            'Bucket' => $bucketName,
            'Key'    => $userId . '/profile_picture',
            'Body'   => fopen($imageFile['tmp_name'], 'r'),
            'ACL'    => 'public-read' // make the image publicly accessible
        ]);

        $db = new PDO('mysql:host=localhost;dbname=test', $user, $pass);
        $sql = "UPDATE users SET profile_picture = :profilePicture WHERE id = :id";
        $stmt = $db->prepare($sql);
        $stmt->bindValue(':profilePicture', $result['ObjectURL']);
        $stmt->bindValue(':id', $userId);
        $stmt->execute();

        // handle potential network interruptions, service outages, and transient errors gracefully using try catch blocks.
    } catch(Aws\S3\Exception\S3Exception $e) {
        throw new Exception('Error uploading image to S3: ' . $e->getMessage());
    } catch (PDOException $e) {
        throw new Exception('Database error: ' . $e->getMessage());
    }
}
?>
```

Please note that this code has a lot of assumptions about your setup. You will need to replace "your-s3-bucket-name", "your-aws-access-key", "your-aws-secret-key" and the PDO connection string with actual values. For simplicity, error handling is kept at a minimum here. 

Also note that image processing in PHP can consume a lot of resources. If you are dealing with large images or need to process many images in parallel, consider using dedicated services like AWS Lambda for image resizing and uploading directly from the client-side application. The modified image should be sent back to your server after being processed on the client side.
']

