['Here is a simple implementation of the requirements above using Rust's standard library and std::fs module to manipulate filesystem paths and file metadata. The cleanup process will be handled by drop method for the struct implementing Drop trait. 

This code doesn't handle all edge cases, error handling is kept at minimum for brevity:

```rust
use std::{env, fs, io, path, process};
use log::info;
use std::sync::OnceLock;

static ROOT_DIR: &str = "/var/app/configs";
static APP_CONFIG_DIR: &str = "APP_CONFIG_DIR";

#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct ConfigDir {
    dir_path: String,
}

impl Drop for ConfigDir {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        let _ = fs::remove_dir_all(&self.dir_path);
    }
}

impl ConfigDir {
    pub fn new(file_name: &str) -> Result<Self, io::Error> {
        Self::initialize()?;

        let dir_path = format![r#"{ROOT_DIR}/{}{}"#, file_name, process::id()];
        if fs::metadata(&dir_path).is_err() {
            info![format![r#"creating directory: {}"#, &dir_path]);
            std::fs::create_dir_all(&dir_path)?;
            env::set_var(APP_CONFIG_DIR, dir_path.as_str())?;
        } else {
            info![format![r#"directory already exists: {}"#, &dir_path]);
        }

        Ok(Self { dir_path })
    }

    pub fn initialize() -> Result<(), io::Error> {
        if let Ok(dir) = env::var(APP_CONFIG_DIR) {
            return Ok(());  // APP_CONFIG_DIR is already set, do nothing
        } else {
            fs::create_dir_all(ROOT_DIR)?;
            Ok(())
        }
    }
}
```
The code above may need further improvements depending on the specific requirements of your application. For example, you might want to add more logging statements or handle different file permissions and ownership scenarios. Also note that this is a simple implementation and doesn't take into account things like security considerations for sensitive data in configuration files. Please make sure to thoroughly test and validate any code produced by AI when used for production purposes.
