['This problem requires a good understanding of multithreading, synchronization primitives like mutexes and condition variables, error checking, and cleanup procedures. Here is the basic code that demonstrates these concepts but there are many things to consider such as how large will our list be, what kind of resources will we manage, etc., so this solution is simplified:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <forward_list>
#include <algorithm>
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mtx; // global mutex for synchronization

int resourceManager(std::forward_list<std::string>& list, int resourceCount, const std::string& str1, const std::string& str2) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); // acquire mutex

    if (resourceCount <= 0) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Insufficient resources" << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    auto it = std::find(list.begin(), list.end(), str1);
    if (it == list.end()) { // If str1 not found, append str2 to end of the list.
        list.push_front(str2);
    } else { // Insert str2 after first occurrence of str1.
        list.insert_after(it, str2);
    }

    // Resource allocation and management code goes here 
    resourceCount--;

    if (resourceCount == 0) {
        std::cout << "Shutdown initiated" << std::endl;
        list.clear();
    } else if(resourceCount < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Resource count is negative, possible resource exhaustion" << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    // Mutex will be automatically released when lock_guard goes out of scope.

    return 0;
}
```

Note that in a real-world scenario we should also consider thread safety for accessing and modifying the `resourceCount`, as it's currently done using `std::mutex` but not atomically. Also, this does not cover concurrent access scenarios due to absence of specific requirements, but handling them can be achieved with synchronization primitives like locks or condition variables.

