['This is quite a comprehensive task, but here's an initial attempt at creating the required C code block that handles the first part of your problem statement: token-based authentication. 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
    char ip[16];
    int timestamp;
    int count;
} RateLimitEntry;

#define MAX_RATELIMIT 50
RateLimitEntry rateLimitTable[MAX_RATELIMIT];
int rateLimitCount = 0;

void checkRateLimit(char* ip) {
    time_t now = time(NULL);

    // Clean up old entries
    for (int i=0; i<rateLimitCount; ++i) {
        if (now - rateLimitTable[i].timestamp > 60 * 60 * 24) { // If entry is older than a day
            memmove(&rateLimitTable[i], &rateLimitTable[i+1], sizeof(RateLimitEntry)*(MAX_RATELIMIT-i-1));
            --rateLimitCount;
        }
    }

    for (int i=0; i<rateLimitCount; ++i) {
        if (!strcmp(ip, rateLimitTable[i].ip)) {
            ++rateLimitTable[i].count;

            // If this IP has requested too many tokens in the last 24hrs
            if (rateLimitTable[i].count > 10) {
                printf("Too many requests from %s, please try again later.\n", ip);
                exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
            }

            return;
        }
    }

    // Add a new entry for this IP
    if (rateLimitCount < MAX_RATELIMIT) {
        strcpy(rateLimitTable[rateLimitCount].ip, ip);
        rateLimitTable[rateLimitCount].timestamp = now;
        rateLimitTable[rateLimitCount++].count = 1;
    } else {
        printf("Too many unique IPs, please try again later.\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
}

char* generateToken() {
    static char buffer[6];
    for (int i=0; i<sizeof(buffer)-1; ++i) 
        buffer[i] = 'A' + rand() % 26; // Generate a random string of 5 characters. This can be replaced with any other token generation mechanism

    return buffer;
}
```
This code snippet defines a `RateLimitEntry` structure to store IP addresses and the number of requests they've made in the last 24 hours, as well as their timestamps. It has two main functions: `checkRateLimit` which checks if a given IP address is allowed to make more requests (it will exit with an error message otherwise), and `generateToken` that generates a random token string of length 5 using the alphabetical characters.
']

['This task requires a considerable amount of code and an extensive knowledge of C programming language. However, here is a simplified version that focuses on memory allocation and initialization part. For handling concurrency issues, we can use pthread library which provides support for multi-threading in C. To document the rationale behind design choices, I'll add comments as appropriate throughout the code.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <complex.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef double complex comp_t;

static pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void* init(size_t size, void** floatArray, void** complexArray) {
    // Acquire the lock before entering critical section
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    *floatArray = malloc(sizeof(float) * size);
    if (*floatArray == NULL) goto error;

    *complexArray = malloc(sizeof(comp_t) * size);
    if (*complexArray == NULL) goto error;

    // Release the lock after critical section, as we don't need it anymore
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return NULL;

error:
    free(*floatArray);
    *floatArray = NULL;

    free(*complexArray);
    *complexArray = NULL;

    // Release the lock if we failed to allocate memory before returning
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    fprintf(stderr, "Error: Failed to allocate memory\n");
    return "Failed to allocate memory";
}
```
For data generation and logging support, a more complex design would be required. Also, handling potential deadlocks or race conditions with this specific code isn't possible as it solely handles one operation (memory allocation) in a critical section. If multiple threads try to access the same resource simultaneously, they will face contention for the lock, potentially leading to slow performance. To handle high concurrency properly, you may need to look into more advanced thread synchronization mechanisms such as semaphores or condition variables depending on your specific needs and constraints. 

Additionally, if you're planning to use this function in a real-time application, it might be worthwhile to consider using low-level memory management techniques or even allocating all the necessary memory upfront for the maximum size anticipated and then just reusing the buffers as needed, to avoid frequent malloc()/free() calls.

Lastly, regarding floating-point precision, C99 standard allows it to be configured at compile time depending on specific needs of a project or system. The default should be fine for most purposes, but in some specific scenarios you may need higher or lower precision, which requires a different approach than what's shown here.
