['Below is a basic example of how you could implement this logging framework. However, keep in mind that this is a simplified version of what a real-world application would require. 

For brevity, I've only included the main parts of this logging library, and I did not include all possible error handling/configuration scenarios which are crucial for production level applications:

```cpp
#include <spdlog/spdlog.h>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
#include <fstream>

class Logger {
public:
    void initLogger(const std::string& configFilePath) {
        nlohmann::json config;
        std::ifstream fileStream(configFilePath);

        if (!fileStream.is_open()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Could not open configuration file: " + configFilePath);
        }

        try {
            fileStream >> config;
        } catch (const nlohmann::json::parse_error& e) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Invalid JSON in configuration file");
        }

        if (!config.contains("logger")) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Missing logger section in config file");
        }

        auto loggerConfig = config["logger"];

        // Create the loggers and set their formats according to the configuration
        for (auto& it : loggerConfig) {
            std::string name = it.value<std::string>("name", "default_log");
            std::string level = it.value<std::string>("level", "info");
            std::vector<spdlog::sink_ptr> sinks;

            // Create the console sink if configured to do so
            if (it["console"].get<bool>()) {
                auto consoleSink = std::make_shared<spdlog::sinks::stdout_color_sink_mt>();
                consoleSink->set_level(spdlog::level::from_str(level));
                sinks.push_back(consoleSink);
            }

            // Create the file sink if configured to do so
            if (it["file"].is_object()) {
                auto filename = it["file"]["name"].get<std::string>();
                auto maxSize = it["file"]["max_size"].get<long long>() * 1024;
                auto maxFiles = it["file"]["max_files"].get<int>();
                auto fileSink = std::make_shared<spdlog::sinks::rotating_file_sink_mt>(filename, maxSize, maxFiles);
                fileSink->set_level(spdlog::level::from_str(level));
                sinks.push_back(fileSink);
            }

            // Create the combined logger with the given sinks
            auto combinedLogger = std::make_shared<spdlog::logger>(name, begin(sinks), end(sinks));
            spdlog::register_logger(combinedLogger);
        }
    }

    void log(const std::string& name, spdlog::level::level_enum level, const std::string& msg) {
        spdlog::get(name)->log(level, msg);
    }
};
```
This is a highly simplified example, real-world logging library would include additional details like:

- Advanced formatting options for log output.
- Integration with external services (like ELK stack).
- Support for asynchronous operations to avoid blocking the main thread.
- Proper handling of error scenarios and recovery strategies.
- Thread safety mechanisms to prevent race conditions and data corruption.
- Robust configuration validation, including schema validation of JSON configuration file.
- Configurable log rotation policies.
- Support for structured logging formats like JSON or key-value pairs.
- Support for dynamic reconfiguration without restarting the application.
- Comprehensive test coverage to ensure correctness and reliability.

For a real-world implementation, consider using an established open-source library that has been battle-tested in production environments, such as `spdlog` or `g3log`. They've done most of the hard work for you already.
']

