['Here is an example implementation of how you could tackle this problem using Java with Guava library. Note that due to the constraints of text-based assistance and the complexity of some components, full code cannot be provided.

```java
import com.google.common.cache.Cache;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheBuilder;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class MD5HashService {
    private final Cache<String, String> cache;
    private Logger logger;
    private MetadataService metadataService;

    public MD5HashService(Logger logger, MetadataService metadataService) {
        this.logger = logger;
        this.metadataService = metadataService;

        // Cache with LRU eviction policy and maximum size of 10_000
        cache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(10_000)
                .expireAfterWrite(30, TimeUnit.MINUTES)
                .build();
    }

    public String computeMD5Hash(String input) {
        // Check string length
        if (input == null || input.length() > 1024) {
            logger.warning("Input string exceeded 1024 characters");
            return null;
        }

        try {
            // Check cache first before computing hash
            String cachedHash = cache.getIfPresent(input);
            if (cachedHash != null) {
                return cachedHash;
            }

            // Compute MD5 hash
            String md5Hex = DigestUtils.md5Hex(input);

            // Update the metadata service asynchronously
            metadataService.updateMetadata(input, md5Hex)
                    .exceptionally(throwable -> {
                        logger.warning("Failed to update metadata: " + throwable);
                        return null;
                    });

            // Put into cache if string length is up to 256 characters
            if (input.length() <= 256) {
                cache.put(input, md5Hex);
            }

            return md5Hex;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.severe("Failed to compute MD5 hash: " + e);
            return null;
        }
    }
}
``` 

In this code, Guava's CacheBuilder is used for the caching mechanism. The maximum cache size is set to 10_000 and the entries expire after 30 minutes of inactivity (you may need to adjust based on your needs). The computeMD5Hash method first checks if the input string is in the cache. If it's not, it computes the MD5 hash and adds it to the cache if the length of the string is up to 256 characters. Finally, it asynchronously updates a metadata service with the computed hash for consistency reasons (note that this will not be successful if the MetadataService throws an exception).

This code is designed in a way that can scale horizontally in a cloud environment because of its stateless nature and the fact that external services are used for caching and logging. However, you may need to design your system to handle such scenarios appropriately depending on the specifics of your application. For instance, if consistency is important, consider using a distributed cache or database with replication to ensure all nodes share the same view of data.
']

["Here's a simple implementation of such a function written in C language. 
Please note that this is a simplified version for understanding purposes and there might be more factors to take into account depending on your specific use-case, like error handling, dependencies etc. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct MAC_PRB_MAP {
    int value;
} MAC_PRB_MAP;

typedef struct MAC_HARQ_PROCESS_ID {
    int value;
} MAC_HARQ_PROCESS_ID;

typedef struct LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE {
    int tag;
    MAC_PRB_MAP* prbMap;
    MAC_HARQ_PROCESS_ID* harqProcessId;
} LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE;

LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE* createMessage(int tag, int prbLowerBound, int prbUpperBound, int harqLowerBound, int harqUpperBound) {
    LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE* message = malloc(sizeof(LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE));
    if (message == NULL) {
        return NULL;  // Memory allocation failed.
    }

    MAC_PRB_MAP* prbMap = malloc(sizeof(MAC_PRB_MAP));
    MAC_HARQ_PROCESS_ID* harqProcessId = malloc(sizeof(MAC_HARQ_PROCESS_ID));
    if (prbMap == NULL || harqProcessId == NULL) {
        free(message);  // Memory allocation failed.
        return NULL;
    }

    message->tag = tag;
    prbMap->value = prbLowerBound + rand() % ((prbUpperBound + 1) - prbLowerBound);
    harqProcessId->value = harqLowerBound + rand() % ((harqUpperBound + 1) - harqLowerBound);

    message->prbMap = prbMap;
    message->harqProcessId = harqProcessId;

    return message;
}

LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE** initializeMessages(int num, int lowerTag, int upperTag, int prbLowerBound, int prbUpperBound, int harqLowerBound, int harqUpperBound) {
    srand(time(0));  // Seed the random number generator.

    LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE** messages = malloc(num * sizeof(LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE*));
    if (messages == NULL) {
        return NULL;  // Memory allocation failed.
    }

    int* usedHarqProcessIds = calloc((harqUpperBound - harqLowerBound + 1), sizeof(int));
    if (usedHarqProcessIds == NULL) {
        free(messages);  // Memory allocation failed.
        return NULL;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < num; ++i) {
        int tag = lowerTag + rand() % ((upperTag + 1) - lowerTag);

        int harqProcessId;
        do {
            harqProcessId = harqLowerBound + rand() % ((harqUpperBound + 1) - harqLowerBound);
        } while (usedHarqProcessIds[harqProcessId - harqLowerBound]);

        usedHarqProcessIds[harqProcessId - harqLowerBound] = 1;

        LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE* message = createMessage(tag, prbLowerBound, prbUpperBound, harqProcessId, harqProcessId);
        if (message == NULL) {
            free(usedHarqProcessIds);  // Memory allocation failed.
            for (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
                free(messages[j]->prbMap);
                free(messages[j]->harqProcessId);
                free(messages[j]);
            }
            free(messages);
            return NULL;  // Memory allocation failed.
        }

        messages[i] = message;
    }

    free(usedHarqProcessIds);
    return messages;
}
```
This code creates an array of LTE_RRC_CU_MESSAGE structures, each containing a unique tag and dynamically allocated PRB_MAP and HARQ_PROCESS_ID fields. The createMessage function is used to initialize a single message structure with random values for the fields, while the initializeMessages function initializes multiple message structures at once by calling createMessage.
"]

