['Here is a C code example implementing the described requirements. Please note that `srand()` function is used to set seed for random number generator, which should be done only once before calling `rand()` function.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct record {
    long* offset;
    int* key;
    int value;
} Record;

void initialize_memory(long num_offsets, long num_keys) {
    srand((unsigned int) time(NULL)); // Seed random number generator.

    // Allocate memory for arrays and check if allocation was successful.
    long* offsets = (long*)malloc(num_offsets * sizeof(long));
    int* keys = (int*)malloc(num_keys * sizeof(int));

    if (offsets == NULL || keys == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        exit(0); // Exit program gracefully.
    }

    Record* records = malloc(sizeof(Record) * num_offsets);
    if (records == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation for records failed.\n");
        free(offsets);
        free(keys);
        exit(0); // Exit program gracefully.
    }

    // Initialize offsets and keys with random values between 0 and 1000.
    for (long i = 0; i < num_offsets; i++) {
        offsets[i] = rand() % 1001;
    }

    for (long i = 0; i < num_keys; i++) {
        keys[i] = rand() % 1001;
    }

    // Initialize records with random values between 1 and 100.
    for(long i = 0; i < num_offsets; i++) {
        records[i].offset = &offsets[i];
        records[i].key = &keys[rand() % num_keys]; // Random key from the keys array.
        records[i].value = rand() % 100 + 1;
    }

    /* Use and free allocated memory here */

    free(records);
    free(offsets);
    free(keys);
}
```
The function `initialize_memory` allocates memory for arrays `offsets`, `keys`, and the array of structures `Record`. The arrays are initialized with random values between 0 and 1000. Each record is then initialized with a pointer to an element in the `offsets` array, a pointer to an element in the `keys` array chosen randomly, and an integer value set to a random value between 1 and 100. If memory allocation fails for any of the arrays, a message is printed indicating failure and then the program exits gracefully.

The function also uses dynamic memory management techniques like allocating and deallocating memory for each array after use, which ensures there are no potential memory leaks or partial allocations. It's important to note that this solution assumes the allocation of all memory at once is feasible within the given performance constraints for large datasets (as `malloc` function call may be costly).
