['Implementing a full Rust application with all of these features would result in quite a bit of code, so here is a simple example of how you could start structuring your program using the Tokio library for asynchronous data processing and integration with command line arguments. This will give you an idea on how to approach designing such system in Rust:

```rust
use tokio::sync::Mutex;
use clap::Parser;
use std::time::Duration;
use tokio::task::spawn_blocking;
use std::thread;
use tokio::net::TcpStream;
use tokio::time::delay_for;
use tokio::sync::Semaphore;

// Define command line arguments and their defaults
#[derive(Parser, Debug)]
struct Args {
    #[clap(short, long, default_value = "5")]
    threads: usize,
    #[clap(long, default_value = "1024")]
    memory_limit: usize,
}

// Dummy data processing function which represents a pipeline stage
async fn process_data(mutex: &Mutex<Vec<i32>>) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let data = mutex.lock().await;

    // Simulate some heavy processing work with a delay
    delay_for(Duration::from_millis(10)).await;

    Ok(())
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let args = Args::parse();
    let concurrency_limit = args.threads as usize;
    let memory_usage_limit = args.memory_limit as usize;

    // Simulate multiple pipelines running in parallel
    let mut semaphore = Semaphore::new(concurrency_limit);
    let shared_data: Mutex<Vec<i32>> = Mutex::new(vec![1, 2, 3]);

    for _i in 0..memory_usage_limit {
        let semaphore = semaphore.clone();
        let shared_data = shared_data.clone();

        // Spawn a new task to process data asynchronously
        spawn_blocking(move || -> std::io::Result<()> {
            runtime::Builder::new_current_thread()
                .enable_all()
                .build()
                .unwrap()
                .block_on(async { 
                    semaphore.acquire().await;
                    process_data(&shared_data).await;
                })
        }).expect("Failed to spawn worker");
    }
}
```
This example only simulates the asynchronous processing of data and does not handle command line arguments, error handling, logging or other advanced features requested in your prompt. 

It is important to note that real-world Rust applications involving network I/O, configuration, CLI parsing, logging and more often than not would be larger programs divided into multiple modules or crates. Each stage of such application would typically have its own dedicated crate including unit tests as well for a complete software development lifecycle (SDLC).
']

