["Here's a basic JavaScript function that uses recursion and event delegation to efficiently traverse the DOM:

```javascript
function getElements(selectors) {
  // Initialize an array to hold our results
  let matches = [];

  // Define our recursive function
  const findMatches = (node) => {
    // Check if this node matches any of our selectors
    for (let i = 0; i < selectors.length; i++) {
      if (node.matches(selectors[i])) {
        matches.push(node);
      }
    }

    // If the node has children, recursively search them
    node = node.firstElementChild;
    while (node) {
      findMatches(node);
      node = node.nextElementSibling;
    }
  };

  // Start searching from the document's body
  findMatches(document.body);

  return matches;
}
```
This function uses a depth-first traversal to visit each element exactly once, ensuring that we don't miss any elements even if they are deeply nested or conditionally loaded. It also takes advantage of the native `matches` method for efficiency. However, it may not accurately report elements that are hidden by CSS rules (`display: none;`), dynamic changes to content, or elements with absolute positioning that are outside the viewport.

