['Here's an initial implementation of the Java utility class that meets your needs. It uses Guava's `CharMatcher` for filtering strings based on specified character sets, Apache Commons' `DigestUtils` for MD5 hashing, and ConcurrentHashMap for concurrency and performance. The configuration is read from a properties file:

```java
import com.google.common.base.CharMatcher;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.util.Properties;
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class StringProcessor {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(StringProcessor.class);

    private CharMatcher charMatcher;
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> hashMap;
    private ExecutorService executor;
    private Properties config;

    public StringProcessor() {
        this.charMatcher = CharMatcher.JAVA_LETTER_OR_DIGIT; // Default character set
        this.hashMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());

        try (InputStream inputStream = getClass().getClassLoader().getResourceAsStream("config.properties")) {
            config = new Properties();
            config.load(inputStream);

            // Check if configuration file specified a different character set
            String charset = config.getProperty("charset");
            if (charset != null && CharMatcher.inRange(charset).countIn(this.charMatcher.toString()) > 0) {
                this.charMatcher = CharMatcher.inRange(charset); // Update character set to the one in the config file
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to load configuration", e);
        }
    }

    public void processStringStream(InputStream inputStream) throws IOException {
        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(inputStream, StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                executor.execute(() -> processString(line));
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to read input stream", e);
        } finally {
            executor.shutdown(); // Shut down the executor service after processing is complete
        }
    }

    private void processString(String str) {
        if (charMatcher.matchesAllOf(str)) { // Check if all characters in the string match the specified character set
            String hash = DigestUtils.md5Hex(str);
            hashMap.putIfAbsent(str, hash);
        } else {
            logger.warn("Skipping string that does not match the required character set: " + str);
        }
    }

    public ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> getHashMap() {
        return hashMap;
    }
}
```
This class reads from an input stream line by line and submits each line to be processed in a separate thread using an `ExecutorService`. It uses Guava's `CharMatcher` to filter strings based on the specified character set, Apache Commons' `DigestUtils` for MD5 hashing, and ConcurrentHashMap for storing results and ensuring concurrency and performance.

The class also loads a configuration file (config.properties) with the desired character set for hashing. If no such file exists or doesn't specify a different character set, it uses `CharMatcher.JAVA_LETTER_OR_DIGIT` as a default. The utility class logs warnings when strings are skipped due to not matching the required character set and handles exceptions by logging error messages.
']

