['Here's a basic implementation of such a utility method using Jackson library for JSON parsing, XStream for XML, and Java Serialization for binary. The versioning is not directly managed within this code as it's typically done at the data model level.

```java
import java.io.*;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.thoughtworks.xstream.XStream;

public class DynamicLoader {
    private static final ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
    private static final XStream xStream = new XStream();

    public enum FormatType { JSON, XML, BINARY }

    private String defaultPath;
    private Class[] allowedClasses;

    public DynamicLoader(String defaultPath, Class...allowedClasses) {
        this.defaultPath = defaultPath;
        this.allowedClasses = allowedClasses;
    }

    public Object loadState(FormatType format, String fileName, Class<?> clazz) throws Exception {
        File file = new File(defaultPath + "/" + fileName);

        if(!isClassAllowed(clazz)) 
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unallowed class");

        switch (format) {
            case JSON: return mapper.readValue(file, clazz);
            case XML : return xStream.fromXML(new FileReader(file), clazz);
            case BINARY: 
                try(ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream(file)))){
                    return in.readObject();
                }
        }

        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported format");
    }

    private boolean isClassAllowed(Class<?> clazz){
       for (Class allowed : allowedClasses) {
           if (allowed.isAssignableFrom(clazz)) 
               return true;
       }
       return false;
    }
}
```
This utility class provides a method `loadState` which accepts file name, format and the class of the expected object to be loaded from the file. It verifies if the class is allowed before loading (as per your requirement) using an array of allowed classes supplied in the constructor. 

The use of Java serialization for binary data ensures a high degree of compatibility with existing systems as most existing objects can be serialized and deserialized with no need to make them implement any additional interfaces or extend any base class, which may complicate refactoring efforts.

Please note that the error handling here is rudimentary and depends on what you want your method to do in case of different issues. You might want to add more specific exceptions or handle edge cases differently depending on your needs.
