<?php
require 'vendor/autoload.php'; // composer autoload
use GuzzleHttp\Client;
use GuzzleHttp\Exception\RequestException;
use Psr\Cache\CacheItemPoolInterface;
use Psr\Log\LoggerInterface;

class AuthManager {
    private $client;
    private $cache;
    private $logger;

    public function __construct(Client $client, CacheItemPoolInterface $cache, LoggerInterface $logger) {
        $this->client = $client;
        $this->cache = $cache;
        $this->logger = $logger;
    }

    // Authenticate and fetch user info using OAuth 2.0
    public function authenticateAndFetchUserInfo(string $accessToken): array {
        $userCacheItemKey = 'user_info_' . $accessToken;
        $cachedUser = $this->cache->getItem($userCacheItemKey);

        if ($cachedUser->isHit()) {
            $this->logger->debug('Fetching user info from cache');
            return $cachedUser->get();
        }

        $response = null;

        try {
            $this->client->post($_ENV['OAUTH_PROVIDER_ENDPOINT'], [
                'headers' => [
                    'Authorization' => 'Bearer ' . $accessToken,
                ],
            ]);

            // Handle response and return user info...
            $userInfo = []; // ...

            $cachedUser->set($userInfo)->expiresAfter(3600); // Cache for 1 hour
            $this->cache->save($cachedUser);

            return $userInfo;
        } catch (RequestException $e) {
            $this->logger->error('Failed to fetch user info', [
                'statusCode' => $e->getResponse()->getStatusCode(),
            ]);

            throw new Exception('Authentication failed'); // Or handle error in a different way...
        } finally {
            $this->logger->info('Authenticating and fetching user info', [
                'userCacheKey' => $userCacheItemKey,
                'statusCode' => $response ? $response->getStatusCode() : null,
            ]);
        }
    }
}
```
This code assumes that you have an OAuth 2.0 provider endpoint in your .env file and a PSR-6 cache pool and logger available for use (which can be fulfilled by libraries like symfony/cache and monolog respectively). It also assumes that the response of the request to this endpoint contains the user information, which is not necessarily true in all cases but it's a simplification.

Remember, this is just a simple skeleton, you would need to add more code depending on how your specific environment works. For example, in production, you should handle the expiration of access tokens and invalidate them accordingly. Also, this code doesn't take into account concurrent requests or thread safety which could be problematic if multiple users are trying to authenticate at the same time. To solve this issue, a proper locking mechanism may need to be implemented.
