["Here's an initial implementation that can be extended further based on your specific requirements. The code is written in C language:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    int valid; // 1 if the structure is valid, 0 otherwise
    void* RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;
    unsigned RNTI;
    unsigned CRCFLAG;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct node *next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t mutexhead = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void push(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T val) {
    Node *new_node = (Node *) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    new_node->data = val;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutexhead);
    new_node->next = head;
    head = new_node;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutexhead);
}

Node* pop() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutexhead);

    if (head == NULL) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutexhead);
        return NULL;
    } else {
        Node *result = head;
        head = head->next;

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutexhead);
        return result;
    }
}
```

This is a simple implementation of a linked list with push and pop operations. It includes an `NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T` structure type, which can be adjusted as needed for your specific use case. The list is thread-safe due to the use of mutexes. Note that this is a basic implementation and does not include error handling, memory management for structures, or garbage collection as per your requirements.
