['The given task requires a detailed understanding of C language and low-level programming. It includes dynamic loading of cryptographic functions which may not be supported directly in standard C. This problem will require a good knowledge of operating system APIs, possibly including POSIX (for Unix/Linux) or Windows APIs for DLL handling. 

Here is an example of how you might implement such functionality using the dlopen and dlsym functions from libdl (which are part of the standard C library). These functions allow you to dynamically load a shared object file (.so on Linux, .dll on Windows) at runtime, and access its symbols.

However, please note that error handling is very basic in this example:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

typedef struct {
    // Define your structure here...
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef void (*hashFunction)(void *data, size_t len, unsigned char* hash);
typedef void* (*cipherFunction)(void *key, size_t keyLen, void *data, size_t dataLen);

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* manageStructures(char *hashLibPath, char *cipherLibPath) {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T *pdu = malloc(sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));

    // Load the hash library
    void *hashHandle = dlopen(hashLibPath, RTLD_LAZY);
    if (!hashHandle) {
        free(pdu);
        return NULL;  // Handle error here...
    }

    // Get a pointer to the hashing function
    hashFunction hashFunc = dlsym(hashHandle, "myHashFunction");
    char *error = dlerror();
    if (error != NULL) {
        free(pdu);
        return NULL;  // Handle error here...
    }

    // Use the hashing function...
    unsigned char hash[32];  // Assume SHA-256 for example
    (*hashFunc)(data, dataLen, hash);

    // Load the cipher library
    void *cipherHandle = dlopen(cipherLibPath, RTLD_LAZY);
    if (!cipherHandle) {
        free(pdu);
        return NULL;  // Handle error here...
    }

    // Get a pointer to the cipher function
    cipherFunction cipherFunc = dlsym(cipherHandle, "myCipherFunction");
    error = dlerror();
    if (error != NULL) {
        free(pdu);
        return NULL;  // Handle error here...
    }

    // Use the cipher function...
    void *encryptedData = (*cipherFunc)(key, keyLen, data, dataLen);
    if (!encryptedData) {
        free(pdu);
        return NULL;  // Handle error here...
    }

    pdu->data = encryptedData;

    return pdu;
}
```
This is a simplified example. In a real world application, you would need to add more checks and balances (e.g., checking if the loaded functions are of the correct types, handling errors from dlopen/dlsym calls, etc.). Also, depending on your system's capabilities, you might want to look into using function pointers in a safer manner than simply casting and calling them directly.
']

