["Here is a basic implementation of what you've requested in C language. This code includes functions to allocate and free memory for an array of `data_item` structures. I have also included error checking for failed memory allocation and added some documentation explaining the design choices made.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

/* Structure definition */
typedef struct {
    char* buffer;
    int value;
} data_item;

/* Function to generate a random number within given range */
int rand_range(int min, int max) {
    return rand() % (max - min + 1) + min;
}

/* Allocate memory for an array of `data_item` structures. 
   This function handles variable number of structures and allocates memory
   for a buffer within each structure based on the specified maximum length */
data_item* allocate_memory(size_t num_items, size_t max_buffer_length) {
    data_item *array = malloc(num_items * sizeof(data_item));

    if (array == NULL) {
        return NULL; /* Failed memory allocation */
    }

    for (size_t i = 0; i < num_items; ++i) {
        array[i].buffer = malloc(max_buffer_length);

        if (array[i].buffer == NULL) {
            /* Failed memory allocation, free previously allocated memory */
            for (size_t j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
                free(array[j].buffer);
            }

            free(array);
            return NULL;
        }

        array[i].value = rand_range(1, 100); /* Random number between 1 and 100 */
    }

    return array;
}

/* Free memory allocated for an array of `data_item` structures */
void free_memory(data_item *array, size_t num_items) {
    if (array != NULL) {
        /* Free buffer in each structure before the array itself */
        for (size_t i = 0; i < num_items; ++i) {
            free(array[i].buffer);
        }

        free(array);
    }
}
```
The design choices made here include:
1. We use `malloc` to dynamically allocate memory for each structure in the array and its buffer, as this is efficient and ensures optimal usage of system resources. 
2. In case any allocation fails, we free all previously allocated memory before returning NULL from our function. This minimizes memory fragmentation and ensures that all allocated memory is properly deallocated if an error occurs during allocation.
3. We use `rand_range` function to generate a random number within given range for each structure's integer value. This ensures a degree of randomness in the data, as opposed to setting it to a constant or using a time-based seed.
4. This design allows our functions to coexist with other components without causing resource contention because they only allocate memory when requested by the user and always properly deallocates all allocated memory once it's no longer needed.
5. We have made necessary trade-offs between memory usage, performance, and system stability based on these design choices. This is a simple implementation, but could be further optimized or modified depending on specific use cases or requirements. For example, we may want to optimize for cache locality or improve memory fragmentation by using memory pools.
"]

