['This task is too broad to write the exact code here. However, I can provide a simple example of how you might start designing such a system in C. Please note that this code lacks many features which are essential for production use (such as thread safety, statistics tracking etc). Also, this doesn't cover any specific challenges like dealing with external dependencies or latency issues.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    // your data here...
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

typedef struct Node {
    int seqNum;
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* pdu;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL; // global list's head
pthread_mutex_t mutexHead = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; 

int createAndAdd(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* pdu) {
    Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (newNode == NULL) return -1; // allocation error

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutexHead); 

    // calculate seqNum based on the current list length
    int len = 0;
    Node* temp = head;
    while(temp != NULL){
        len++;
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    newNode->seqNum = len + 1; // assuming seqNum starts from 1

    newNode->pdu = pdu; 
    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutexHead);

    return newNode->seqNum;
}
```
This is a very basic example of how you can start to manage your dynamically allocated list. The "createAndAdd" function will add a given NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T structure to the head of the list, assigning it an increasing sequence number based on the length of the list. It uses a mutex to ensure thread safety and avoids race conditions when multiple threads are trying to modify the list at the same time.
