void initRecordList(RecordList* list, size_t capacity) {
    list->capacity = capacity;
    list->size = 0;
    pthread_mutex_init(&list->mutex, NULL);
    list->records = (Record**)malloc(capacity * sizeof(Record*));

    if(list->records == NULL){
        perror("Failed to allocate memory for records");
        exit(-1);
    }
}

// Add a new record, and resize the list if necessary
void addRecord(RecordList* list, Record* record) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&list->mutex);

    // If capacity is exceeded, double it with realloc
    if (list->size >= list->capacity) {
        size_t newCapacity = list->capacity * 2;
        Record** newRecords = realloc(list->records, newCapacity * sizeof(Record*));

        // Check for allocation failure again
        if (newRecords == NULL) {
            perror("Failed to resize the list of records");
            exit(-1);
        }

        list->capacity = newCapacity;
        list->records = newRecords;
    }

    // Add the record and increment size
    list->records[list->size] = record;
    list->size++;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&list->mutex);
}

// Clean up memory for all records in the list
void freeRecordList(RecordList* list) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&list->mutex);

    // Free each record and then the list of pointers
    for (size_t i = 0; i < list->size; ++i) {
        free(list->records[i]);
    }
    free(list->records);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&list->mutex);
}
```
This is a basic structure and it's important to note that this code does not contain any error handling for file I/O operations, data validation, or any other advanced feature. It also doesn't include any load from external sources functionality. The design of the RecordList struct was chosen in order to have an array of pointers so we can resize it and still keep all the records easily accessible through pointers. 

